---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArticleHero from '../../components/ArticleHero.astro';
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Hairstyle Ideas by Face Shape, Length & Type"
  description="Browse hairstyle guides organized by face shape, hair length, hair type and audience."
  canonicalPath="/hairstyles/"
>
  <ArticleHero eyebrow="Hairstyle Ideas" title="Hairstyles" image="/images/gallery/gallery-blonde-waves-1.jpg" imageAlt="Long blonde balayage waves" />
  <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
    <p class="text-xs text-ink/50 mb-2"><a href="/" class="hover:underline">Home</a> / Hairstyles</p>
    <p class="text-ink/70 mb-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
      Browse hairstyle ideas organized however works best for you — by face shape,
      length, type, or audience.
    </p>
    <p class="text-ink/70 mb-8 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
      Finding the right hairstyle usually means balancing a few things at once:
      your face shape, your hair's natural texture, how much time you want to
      spend styling day to day, and whatever look you're personally drawn to. None
      of these guides are meant to be prescriptive — think of them as a starting
      point to bring into a consultation, where a stylist can adapt any of these
      ideas to your specific hair rather than applying them exactly as written.
    </p>
    <p class="text-ink/70 mb-8 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
      Each guide below covers a different way of thinking about hairstyles — by
      the shape of your face, by how short or long you're willing to go, or by
      audience-specific considerations like the vocabulary used in barbering
      versus general styling terms. If you're not sure where to start, face
      shape is usually the most useful entry point, since it applies regardless
      of your current length or texture and gives you a vocabulary to bring into
      any consultation, with any stylist, for any future cut.
    </p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-5">
      <Card title="By Face Shape" description="Find flattering cuts for oval, round, square, heart, diamond and oblong faces." href="/hairstyles/by-face-shape/" image="/images/gallery/gallery-blonde-waves-2.jpg" imageAlt="Hairstyle framing the face" />
      <Card title="Short Hairstyles" description="Ideas and upkeep tips for short cuts." href="/hairstyles/short-hairstyles/" image="/images/gallery/gallery-mens-fade-collage.jpg" imageAlt="Short, precisely cut hairstyle" />
      <Card title="Men's Hairstyles" description="Popular men's cuts and how to talk to your barber or stylist." href="/hairstyles/mens-hairstyles/" image="/images/gallery/gallery-private-studio-1.jpg" imageAlt="Barbershop-style studio chair" />
      <Card title="Women's Hairstyles" description="Trending and timeless women's hairstyle ideas." href="/hairstyles/womens-hairstyles/" image="/images/gallery/gallery-blonde-waves-1.jpg" imageAlt="Long, styled wavy hair" />
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-3">How to Use These Guides Well</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/70 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The most common mistake when researching a new hairstyle is collecting
      reference photos without thinking about how they'll translate to your own
      hair. A style that looks effortless in a photo might rely on a hair
      texture, density, or length very different from yours. Use these guides
      to understand the underlying logic of a style — why it works for certain
      face shapes or hair types — rather than just the finished look, and bring
      that understanding into your consultation alongside any photos you've
      collected.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Hairstyles Change With Hair Health</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
      Keep in mind that a style's success also depends on your hair's current
      condition, not just its natural texture. Heavily damaged hair may not hold
      a style the way healthy hair of the same type would, which is one more
      reason our <a href="/hair-care/" class="text-rose-600 underline">hair care guides</a>
      are worth pairing with these style guides — the healthier your hair, the
      more styles will actually work the way you expect them to.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Revisit as Your Hair Changes</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
      Hair texture and thickness can shift over the years due to age, hormones,
      chemical processing, or simply growing it out after a long time short.
      A style that didn't suit you a few years ago might work beautifully now,
      and it's worth revisiting these guides periodically rather than assuming
      your options are fixed.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Combine These Guides for the Best Result</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
      The most satisfying results usually come from reading more than one of
      these guides together — face shape plus hair type, for instance, rather
      than either alone — since a style that's technically flattering for your
      face but fights your natural texture every day rarely stays your favorite
      for long. Cross-reference before you commit to a big change.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Trends Will Keep Changing</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
      What's trending will inevitably shift again, but the underlying
      principles in these guides — proportion, texture, and how a cut
      interacts with your specific features — stay useful regardless of
      whatever style is currently popular. Use trend-driven guides for
      inspiration, and the more foundational ones for lasting decisions.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Still Not Sure? Start With a Consultation</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
      If you've read through several guides and still aren't sure what
      direction to take, that's a completely normal place to be — and it's
      exactly what a paid consultation, separate from the cut itself, is for.
      Many stylists offer a short consultation appointment specifically to
      talk through options before committing to a chair-side decision.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Bookmark This Page</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
      Hairstyle preferences change more often than most other parts of a hair
      routine — new season, new haircut inspiration, a change in lifestyle.
      Bookmarking this hub, rather than a single style guide, means you'll
      always land somewhere useful the next time you're considering a change.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
